 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "S_market",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Silk Road was an online black market that ran as a hidden service on the Tor network from early 2011 (the complaint: activity from about January/February 2011) until the FBI seized the site and arrested its operator on 1-2 October 2013; buyers paid in bitcoin. Narration: 'a dark-web drug empire built on anonymity' / 'It ran on Tor and Bitcoin'. No drugs or listings are drawn.",
   "sources": [
    "aljazeera_2013",
    "techcrunch_2013",
    "npr_2013",
    "cnn_2013",
    "register_2013"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_sales",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The Justice Department's 2015 sentencing release says Silk Road's users bought and sold more than $200 million in illegal goods and that Ulbricht took commissions of more than $13 million; prosecutors' trial estimate was more than $213 million (2011-2013). The 2013 FBI complaint had converted about 9.5 million bitcoins of revenue to roughly $1.2 billion at then-current exchange rates (CBS; Fox): a conversion, not a measured dollar sum, so narration and the card use the DOJ figure 'over $200 million'.",
   "sources": [
    "doj_2015",
    "dea_2015",
    "networkworld_sent",
    "cbs_2013",
    "fox_2013"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_dpr",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "CARD ONLY: the site's operator was known to investigators only by the handle 'Dread Pirate Roberts' (DPR).",
   "sources": [
    "doj_2015",
    "techcrunch_2013",
    "npr_2013",
    "ice_2015"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_google",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "IRS criminal-investigation special agent Gary Alford (not named on screen), working with the DEA/strike force in New York, searched Google for the earliest mentions of Silk Road and found that in January 2011, shortly after the site started, a user called 'altoid' had posted about Silk Road on the Bitcointalk.org and Shroomery.org forums (NYT, Dec 2015; his trial testimony, Jan 2015). Narration: 'An IRS agent started with a Google search.' / 'A 2011 forum post by altoid advertised Silk Road.' The screen is a generic, illegible forum page.",
   "sources": [
    "nyt_alford",
    "sophos_alford",
    "vice_alford",
    "networkworld_alford",
    "techcrunch_week3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_email",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In altoid's posting history, a later Bitcointalk post seeking a developer (its exact date not in any extract) told applicants to email an address at Gmail that was Ross Ulbricht's own name; with a search warrant the IRS then obtained the account's email from Google. Narration: 'Later, altoid gave his own Gmail address: Ross Ulbricht.' The address itself is never spoken or shown (card: 'A GMAIL ADDRESS · HIS OWN NAME').",
   "sources": [
    "nyt_alford",
    "sophos_alford",
    "vice_alford",
    "networkworld_alford"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_ulbricht",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Ross William Ulbricht, then 29, of San Francisco (originally from Austin, Texas), was charged in Manhattan federal court as the site's operator; he is drawn only as an anonymous silhouette (characters/figures/sr32_ulbricht.json).",
   "sources": [
    "kqed_2013",
    "officer_ap_2013",
    "npr_2013",
    "doj_2015"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_encrypt",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Agents wanted to arrest him with his laptop open and unencrypted: FBI computer scientist Thomas Kiernan testified 'We waited for an opportunity to arrest the defendant with his laptop open to avoid encryption'; the undercover agent (Jared Der-Yeghiayan) said the aim was to get the computer 'in an open, unencrypted state'; closing the lid would have encrypted its contents. Narration: 'Close the lid and it would encrypt. Agents needed it open.'",
   "sources": [
    "pcworld_kiernan",
    "computerworld_kiernan",
    "wired_undercover",
    "yahoo_fight"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_fight",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On 1 October 2013, in the science-fiction section of the Glen Park branch of the San Francisco Public Library, Kiernan stood behind Ulbricht to photograph his screen while two other agents posed as a bickering couple to create a diversion; when he looked up, agents grabbed the laptop before he could close it, and he was arrested without resisting. Narration: 'two agents staged a loud fight behind him' / 'He looked up. An agent grabbed the laptop'. Headline: 'A FAKE FIGHT IN A LIBRARY / CAUGHT THE SILK ROAD FOUNDER'. The agents are generic and unnamed on screen.",
   "sources": [
    "pcworld_kiernan",
    "computerworld_kiernan",
    "yahoo_fight",
    "kqed_2013",
    "officer_ap_2013",
    "police1_2013",
    "wired_undercover"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_admin",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Photographs of the screen showed him logged into Silk Road's 'mastermind' administrator panel as Dread Pirate Roberts; at that moment he was chatting with an undercover agent over the site's staff chat; the laptop's one account, 'Frosty', was signed in. Narration: 'still logged in as the admin.' Card: 'LOGGED IN AS THE ADMIN · DREAD PIRATE ROBERTS'. The screen drawn is a generic dark panel with illegible blocks.",
   "sources": [
    "vice_laptop",
    "pcworld_kiernan",
    "wired_undercover",
    "cbc_caught"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_verdict",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On Wednesday 4 February 2015 a federal jury in Manhattan convicted Ulbricht on all seven counts (including narcotics trafficking, continuing criminal enterprise, computer hacking and money-laundering conspiracies) after less than a day of deliberation.",
   "sources": [
    "nbc_reuters_verdict",
    "npr_verdict",
    "itv_verdict",
    "doj_2015"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_sentence",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On Friday 29 May 2015 U.S. District Judge Katherine B. Forrest sentenced him to life in prison without the possibility of parole (two life terms plus 20, 15 and 5 years, all concurrent). Narration: 'life without parole.'",
   "sources": [
    "doj_2015",
    "wired_sent",
    "coindesk_sent",
    "siliconrepublic_sent",
    "networkworld_sent"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "S_pardon",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On 21 January 2025 President Donald Trump announced a 'full and unconditional pardon' of Ross Ulbricht (after 11 years in prison), and he was released from the federal prison in Tucson, Arizona. Narration states it as fact only: 'In 2025 he was pardoned, and released.' No opinion is given; the burned-in end question asks the viewer.",
   "sources": [
    "washtimes_pardon",
    "ap_pardon",
    "aljazeera_pardon",
    "yahoo_release"
   ]
  }
 ],
